    "body": "Using templates Templates do the design work so you can focus on the story. This page covers working with templates while you build. (New to templates? Start with Understanding templates .) Browsing the gallery On the Reports page, click Add new → From a template . The gallery opens on All Templates plus three entry points — Fundraising, Donor Relations & Stewardship, and Communications — over shelves grouped by category. Any templates your team has saved sit first, under Your templates . Each shelf scrolls sideways. Drag it, or use the arrows at either end. Previewing before you commit Click a template to open it as a real report rather than a thumbnail, so you can scroll it the way a reader would. Beside the preview, Colors and Fonts cards choose what the template is built with. Your default palette and font pair are preselected, so the preview already shows the template in your brand. Click Template colors or Template fonts (marked As designed ) to compare it with the original. A template that looks wrong in your colors is usually telling you something about the colors, not the template. See Starting a report on brand . Starting from a template When you create a report from a template, you get a complete editable copy: cover, sections, layouts, and sample content. From there, building is mostly replacing — your words for the sample words, your photos for the stock photos. Make a template yours Work through the template in this order: Start on brand. Set up your brand kit before you create the report, and keep your palette and font pair selected when you click Use this template . The copy opens already in your colors and fonts. Then drop in your logo from Assets → Your Logos in the builder. Replace content section by section. Keep the layouts; swap the words and images. Prune and add. Delete what doesn't apply and add the blocks the template didn't include. Don't fight the layout. If a block's structure doesn't fit your content, it's usually faster to delete it and add a better-suited block type than to bend it into shape. Keep the sample content until the end Sample text shows you the intended length and tone for each spot — a heading meant for five words reads badly with twenty. Replace content piece by piece rather than clearing everything first, so you always have the design's \"intent\" in view. Reusing your own work Your best template is often last year's report. Duplicate a past report from the dashboard (open the report's ⋯ menu and choose Duplicate ) and update it — structure, branding, and recurring sections (letters, donor lists, financials) carry over, and you only rewrite what changed. To turn a report into a template your whole team can start from, open it in the builder, choose Save report as template from the ⋯ menu, and give it a name. It appears in the gallery under Your templates ."
