    "body": "Report palettes A report palette sets the starting colors of your reports. It's a set of five colors that decides what color the buttons are, what the dark sections look like, and what the light sections look like. Your default palette is applied to every new report, and you can pick a different saved palette when you create one. Storyraise checks every palette for readability before you save it, so a report built on it is easy to read. A palette's colors are its own, separate from your brand colors . They can be brand colors, darker or lighter shades of them, or any other color you pick. Adding or removing a brand color doesn't change your palettes. You'll find palettes on the Brand tab of the brand kit, under Report palettes . Each card previews the palette on a small sample — a dark section with a Give again button, then a light section of stats with a Give button. The strip underneath shows all five colors. The chip under the name tells you whether it's readable: Text passes AA means both required contrast checks (below) pass. What \"AA\" means: AA is the standard level of the web accessibility guidelines (WCAG). A contrast ratio measures how different two colors are, from 1:1 (identical) to 21:1 (black on white). Text needs at least 4.5:1 to be comfortable to read. The five colors in a palette Color Where it's used Buttons Every button in the report. The button's text color is chosen automatically — whichever of the lightest or darkest palette color reads better on it. Dark sections The background of dark sections, and the text color on light sections. In the builder, a block set to Primary uses this. Light sections The background of light sections, and the text color on dark sections. In the builder, a block set to Secondary uses this. Light accent Small decorative touches some templates use, such as shapes and generated backgrounds. Dark accent Small decorative touches some templates use, such as shapes and generated backgrounds. The two accents live under Template accents in the palette editor. Most of the time you only need to think about the first three. Start from an idea You don't have to build a palette by hand. Storyraise suggests palettes that use only your brand colors (and darker or lighter versions of them), and every suggestion passes the contrast checks. Ideas are named for what they do. \"Green on navy\" means green buttons on navy sections, and \"Navy sections\" keeps the sections in your exact navy and adjusts the buttons. The line under each idea tells you which colors it uses exactly and which it has darkened or lightened. Save palette saves the idea as it is. Customize opens it in the palette editor so you can change it first. Before you have any palettes, you'll see up to three ideas. Afterwards, one idea stays on the page, labeled Idea: , in case you want another. Ideas only appear if at least one brand color isn't white, black, or gray. Create or edit a palette Click New palette , or choose Edit from a palette's ⋮ menu. A new palette starts with colors taken from your brand colors, which you can then change. To start from a suggestion instead, click one of the chips under Or start from an idea , then adjust it. Pick a color for each role. Click Buttons , Dark sections , or Light sections on the left to open a color picker with your brand colors at the top. The preview on the right updates as you go, and the section you're editing is highlighted. Open Template accents to set the two accent colors. Watch the checks. Each role shows its contrast ratio, and the four checks under the preview show how the palette does overall (see below). Use the suggestion if something fails. When a check fails, a Suggested for… line offers the smallest lighter or darker change that fixes it. Click Use to apply it. After Use , every role passes: Name it. Type a name (up to 60 characters). If you leave it blank, it's saved as \"Untitled palette\". Tick Use for new reports to make it your default palette, then click Save palette . The default is preselected under Colors whenever someone creates a report or opens a template, and it's the palette shown under New reports start with . Your first palette becomes the default automatically. If you have a default font pair, the preview uses it. Untick Show in your fonts (for example, Show in Fraunces + Karla ) to see the colors on their own. Nothing is saved until you click Save palette . Saving never changes existing reports. Contrast checks Every palette is checked against the WCAG accessibility guidelines (see the note on \"AA\" near the top of this page). The four checks sit under the preview in the palette editor: Check Needs If it fails Text on dark sections 4.5:1 Required. You can't save until it passes Button text 4.5:1 Required. You can't save until it passes Buttons stand out on dark 3:1 Advisory, shown as Low . You can still save Buttons stand out on light 3:1 Advisory, shown as Low . You can still save The \"Text on dark sections\" check also covers dark text on light sections, because it compares the same two colors. A check shows its ratio and a result, such as 10.7:1 Pass , 3.5:1 Fails , or 2.3:1 Low . If you click Save palette while a required check fails, Storyraise moves you to the failing check instead of saving. Tip: A \"Low\" on Buttons stand out on light is common with mid-tone brand colors and is usually fine. The button text is still readable; the button just blends a little more into a white section. Manage your palettes Open the ⋮ menu on a palette card: Edit — change the colors or rename it. Make default — new reports start with this palette. (It doesn't appear on the palette that's already the default.) You can also change the default from Change at the top of the brand kit. Duplicate — makes a copy named \" name copy\", a quick way to try a variation. Delete — removes it from the brand kit. Reports that already use those colors keep them, because every report holds its own copy. The first palette you save becomes the default automatically. If you delete the default, the oldest remaining palette takes over. How a palette shows up in a report When a report starts from a palette (see Starting a report on brand ), its colors become that report's own Report palette : When you color an element, its color picker shows a Report palette row with those five colors, followed by Your brand colors and then the presets. When you select a block (a section of the report), Set Block Style offers Primary (a dark section background with light text) and Secondary (a light section background with dark text). Switching between them is how you alternate dark and light sections. Buttons use the Buttons color, with text in whichever palette color reads better. The + and − at the end of Your brand colors add the color you've picked to your brand kit, or remove the selected brand color. That change is saved to the brand kit straight away, for the whole team. Removing a brand color here removes it for everyone. Use a palette in a report you've already built Changing a palette in the brand kit doesn't touch existing reports. To bring a palette into one: Open the report in the builder. Open ⋯ → Styles (or Manage styles in the Blocks panel). Under Color Palette , click More color palettes . Pick a palette under Your Palettes . Your brand kit palettes are listed first, above the stock palettes under Other Palettes . The whole report is recolored. Any typography you've already fine-tuned, such as font sizes, weights, and line heights, is kept. The report gets its own copy of the colors, so later edits in the report don't change the brand kit palette, and the other way round. Related Brand kit Font pairs Starting a report on brand Fonts and colors Accessibility considerations"
