    "body": "Font pairs A font pair is a heading font and a body font that belong together. Save one or more pairs in your brand kit, mark one as the default, and every new report starts in those fonts. You'll find them on the Brand tab of the brand kit, under Font pairs . Each card shows a heading and a short paragraph in the pair's own fonts, so you can compare pairs at a glance. Coming from primary and secondary fonts? Font pairs replace the old primary and secondary brand fonts. If your organization had set them, they were turned into a pair called Your fonts and made the default the first time an Owner, Admin, or Editor opened the brand kit. Nothing changes in your existing reports. Your first pair If you haven't saved a pair yet, the section suggests one. If you've uploaded font files, the suggestion comes from them. Otherwise it's a classic serif-and-sans combination. Click Save this pair to take it as is, or Choose other fonts to change it first. Create or edit a pair Click New pair , or choose Edit from a pair's ⋮ menu. Under Headings , click the font name to choose a family. Pick a style from the menu under it. Storyraise starts you on a sensible weight — bold for headings, regular for body text — and only lists the weights that family actually has. Do the same under Body text . Check the preview on the right. It shows the pair in your default palette. The Name fills itself in (for example, \"Fraunces + Karla\") until you type your own, up to 60 characters. Tick Use for new reports to make it your default pair, then click Save pair . The default is preselected under Fonts whenever someone creates a report or opens a template. Your first pair becomes the default automatically. Finding a font The font list shows your uploaded fonts first, then every Google font from A to Z, each written in its own typeface. Type in Search all fonts… to narrow it down. Use the ↑ and ↓ keys to try each font in the preview without committing. Press Esc to go back to the font you had. Manage your pairs Open the ⋮ menu on a pair: Edit — change the fonts, styles, or name. Make default — new reports start with this pair. You can also change the default from Change at the top of the brand kit. Delete — asks Delete this pair? on the card. Click Delete to confirm or Keep to cancel. The first pair you save becomes the default automatically. If you delete the default, the oldest remaining pair takes over. Changing or deleting a pair doesn't change existing reports. Upload your own fonts If your brand font isn't on Google Fonts, upload the font files. At the bottom of the Font pairs section, click Upload font files . Choose one or more .ttf , .otf , .woff , or .woff2 files. You can select a whole family's files at once. Click Font files to expand the list and check that each file appears under the right family name. Click New pair (or Edit on an existing pair) and pick your uploaded font. Uploaded fonts are listed above the Google fonts. Storyraise groups the files by the family name stored inside each font, not by file name. \"Brand-Bold.otf\" and \"brand_regular.ttf\" end up together if they're the same family, with one chip per style. Uploaded fonts then appear at the top of the font list in the pair editor. In the builder's font lists they're also listed first, by file name. The list also helps you tidy up: Exact copies. If you've uploaded the same file twice, the family says so, and Hide copies hides the extras from the brand kit's font picker. Nothing is deleted. No family name. Some files don't name their family. They're grouped under No family name until you click Name family and give them one. Removing a font file Click the × on a style's chip. The confirmation tells you exactly what will go: The file is deleted, and it's cleared from your brand defaults. Any font pair that uses that font family is deleted too , even if you're only removing one of its styles. The dialog lists those pairs by name before you confirm. Warning: This is the one brand kit change that can reach existing reports. Reports load uploaded fonts from your uploads, so a report that uses a removed font can fall back to a different font. Upload the replacement and switch those reports to it before you remove the old file. Fonts in the builder A report gets its fonts when it's created: the pair you choose in the Fonts cards becomes that report's heading and body fonts (see Starting a report on brand ). After that, the report's fonts are set in the builder: ⋯ → Styles sets the report-wide Heading Font and Body Font . Your uploaded fonts are listed first. Each text element's own font, weight, and size can be adjusted in its settings — see Fonts and colors . Brand kit pairs aren't listed in the builder, so to give an existing report your pair's fonts, set Heading Font and Body Font to match it. Tips Pair contrast with calm. A distinctive heading font next to a quiet, very readable body font almost always works. Keep body text simple. Script and display faces look great in a big heading and are tiring to read in a paragraph. One default, a few alternates. A second pair for appeals or event invitations is useful. Ten pairs mean nobody knows which to pick. Related Brand kit Report palettes Starting a report on brand Fonts and colors"
