    "body": "Adding a poll A poll asks your readers a question inside the report and shows the answers as a bar chart. It's the quickest way to turn a report from something people read into something they take part in. Answers collect under Poll Results in that report's Analytics, where you can see them per person and export them. Want readers to find out whether they were right? The same element can be a quiz — see Adding a quiz . Adding a poll In the builder, open the Insert bar and choose Poll/Quiz . Click the poll to open the Poll Editor . On the Question & Answers tab, leave Poll selected at the top. Type your question and fill in the answers. Click Done . A new poll starts empty on purpose: no sample question, no sample answers. An unedited poll shows nothing at all to a reader, so a poll you forgot to finish can never reach a donor with placeholder wording in it. You can also add a whole Poll/Quiz block from the block list ( Add a block ), which gives the poll a section of its own rather than sitting it inside another block. Answers A poll needs at least two answers and takes up to ten. Past ten the bars stop being readable stacked in a column, so the editor won't add more. Each answer gets an accent color, seeded from a set that works together. You can change any of them. Tip: Keep answers short. Once any answer passes 32 characters, the Automatic layout moves every answer's text inside its bar instead of beside it. The editor warns you on the answer that triggers it. Settings The Poll Editor has three tabs: Question & Answers , Voting , and Appearance . Use Preview in the editor's header to try the poll before you place it. How it behaves Question — Shown or Hidden , at the bottom of the Question & Answers tab. Hide it when the surrounding copy already asks the question, so a reader isn't asked twice. Selection — Single choice for one answer per reader, Multiple choice to let them pick several. Changing answers — Readers can change their answer , or One response only to lock in a reader's first answer. After voting — Show everyone the results reveals the full tally once someone answers. Confirm their answer only shows them what they chose and keeps everyone else's answers to you. How it reads Show values as — counts, percentages, or both. Value position — after the bar, inside it, or hidden. Answers/Options text (on the Appearance tab) — Automatic keeps short answers beside their bar and moves long ones inside it. You can also force Beside the bar , Above the bar , or Inside the bar . Font, text color, bar height, roundness, and spacing are all on the Appearance tab, and brand kit colors appear in every color picker. What readers see A reader picks an answer and the bars animate to the result straight away. Nothing waits on the network, so it feels instant even on a slow connection. Votes are only recorded on the published report. Answering a poll while you're proofing in the builder, in preview, in print view, or in a PDF export does not count — a poll you clicked through while checking your work will never appear in your results. Seeing the results Open the report's Analytics and choose the Poll Results tab. It appears on any report that contains a poll, and it's called Polls & Quizzes once the report has a quiz in it. The Results → Analytics link at the bottom of the editor's Question & Answers tab takes you straight there once the report is published. For each poll you get the question, the tally per answer, and a list of who answered. A reader who opened a personalized link is named; anyone else shows as Anonymous . Search the list by name or answer, or filter it to one answer. Use Download CSV on a poll to download its votes: one row per answer, with the question, the answer, and the respondent's name and constituent ID. Note: Because a personalized link identifies the reader, poll answers on a personalized report are attributable to a named constituent. Say so in the question if the answer is something people would expect to give anonymously. Turn it into a quiz At the top of the Question & Answers tab, choose Quiz instead of Poll . You mark the correct answer, and each reader finds out whether they got it right, with an optional explanation to follow. See Adding a quiz . Related Adding a quiz Editing content Adding sections Personalized links Analytics overview"
