    "keywords": [
      "scenes",
      "personalization",
      "storyraise",
      "video"
    ],
    "body": "Scenes and personalization Scenes are the on-screen text that appears over your video as you speak — and they're where each recipient's message becomes personally theirs. What scenes are When you finish recording, Storyraise automatically structures your video into a few scenes — timed text overlays with animated headings, sized and styled to match. Each scene appears at the right moment as your video plays. Editing a scene Under Edit scenes , your scenes appear as blocks on a timeline under the video. Click a block to select that scene, and drag its left or right edge to change when it starts or ends. With a scene selected you can: Change its words under Edit Text . Describe a change under Refine with AI (for example, \"shorter, bolder\") and click Regenerate scene . Click Insert icon to drop in a visual accent, or Insert variable to add a merge tag. The ⋯ menu beside the timeline holds the rest. To add a scene, play the video to the moment you want it, open the menu, and click Add scene at — the option carries the current timestamp, so the new scene lands where you're paused rather than at the end. Generating it takes a moment. The same menu has Duplicate scene , and Remake all scenes when you want a fresh start on the overlay design. Personalizing with merge tags This is what sets Storyraise Video apart. Scene text supports merge tags in the @@field_name@@ format, so: Hi, @@first_name@@! becomes \"Hi, Maria!\" for Maria and \"Hi, James!\" for James — automatically, from each recipient's record. Any field on your constituents can be a merge tag: @@first_name@@ , @@last_name@@ , or custom fields like giving level or program name. The same ?for= personalization that powers personalized report links drives video personalization. Merge tags fill in from the video's own recipients — the people you added with Select Recipients or a CSV (see Sending and recipients ), matched by email. Someone who isn't one of the video's recipients sees the video with the merge tags left blank. Tips Put the name on screen early. Seeing their own name in the first scene tells the viewer instantly that this was made for them. Keep overlay text short. Scenes punctuate your spoken words — a few words land; a paragraph competes with you. Check your data first. A recipient missing a field a scene uses gets a blank where their name should be. Preview before sending — see Sending and recipients . Next Sending and recipients — choose who gets the video and preview each person's version."
