    "body": "Salesforce / Nonprofit Cloud Connect Salesforce to bring your contacts and giving data into Storyraise — and send report engagement back to Salesforce so it lives next to the rest of each donor's record. What syncs Into Storyraise Contacts → your Storyraise constituent list , matched by email. Name and email always come across; you can pick extra Contact fields at connect time, and each one becomes a merge tag. Contacts without an email are skipped. Giving and other objects → Storyraise collections. Storyraise discovers your org's objects and fields at connect time, so you choose exactly what to bring in. This includes Nonprofit Cloud Gift Transactions — Storyraise resolves each gift's Person Account to the right constituent automatically. See Syncing data to collections . Back into Salesforce Report engagement (opens, reports viewed, minutes engaged, link clicks, and whether the donor is a returning visitor) → logged on each donor's Contact, so your team sees Storyraise activity inside Salesforce. You choose where it lands and who it goes to (see below). Connect Salesforce uses sign-in (OAuth), so there's no API key to copy: In the Connections tab, find Salesforce / Nonprofit Cloud and click Connect . You're sent to Salesforce to sign in and authorize Storyraise. Approve access — you'll return to Storyraise, and a Who should sync from Salesforce? picker opens. Choose Everyone (every Contact the connection can see), or tick one or more of your Salesforce Contact List Views . A contact who appears in two views is only imported once. Optionally, under Bring extra fields across , pick any Contact fields you want beyond name and email. Each field you pick becomes a merge tag you can use in reports. Click Sync constituents . Sign in with a Salesforce account that can see the contacts you want to bring over — and, for write-back, that has permission to create Tasks or edit the Contact fields you choose below. Send engagement to Salesforce On the connected Salesforce row, choose Send to Salesforce to configure write-back: Where to write it — Activity logs a completed Task on the Contact; Contact fields updates custom fields on the Contact (you map each Salesforce field to a metric: opens, reports, minutes, link clicks, engagement score, returning visitor, or last open). Activity subject — the subject on each Task. Defaults to Storyraise report engagement . Assign the task to each donor's gift officer (Account Owner) — optionally assign each Task to the donor's Account Owner, so the right fundraiser is notified. Minimum engagement to send — Everyone with any activity , Opened a report (1★+) , Engaged — read in depth (2★+) , or High intent — clicked a link (3★) . Click Save to keep the settings, or Save & send now to send right away. Once saved, engagement is also sent on every scheduled sync while Auto-sync is on. Each donor gets one Storyraise Task, updated in place as their engagement changes — no duplicate Tasks pile up on the Contact. If someone deletes that Task in Salesforce, the next send creates a fresh one. Only constituents who came from Salesforce and have recorded engagement are sent. Engagement is matched to the exact Contact using a Storyraise constituent id carried through each personalized report link, not by name — so it lands on the right person. Keeping it current Click the sync icon on the connection row ( Sync now ) to run a sync immediately — the Who should sync from Salesforce? picker reopens, so you can confirm or change the scope. Or open the row's ⋯ More menu, turn on Auto-sync (it's off by default), and set the Frequency to Daily or Weekly (weekly syncs run on Mondays). When write-back is saved, auto-sync also sends engagement on the same schedule. Each sync re-pulls everyone in the chosen scope and updates existing records in place. Contacts deleted in Salesforce are not removed from Storyraise — remove them in Storyraise yourself if they shouldn't stay. See Managing connections . Related The Connections tab Syncing data to collections Troubleshooting sync issues"
